      "content": "that's an interesting way to spin it. but i need to be careful—we can't have the reps actually claiming it's a \"sign of potency\" if the clinical studies don't explicitly state that. if a doctor calls our bluff and asks for the specific mechanism or data that links dizziness to efficacy, we're going to look bad and lose credibility. \n\nfor the sake of the reps' confidence, can we refine this? i want them to be able to tell the doctor that the dizziness is actually a result of the drug's superior absorption rate compared to the competitor, which is why it's more effective. does that sound like a plausible way to justify the data while keeping the focus on the drug's benefits?",
